I have an Excel file called [YOUR_FILE_NAME].xlsx in the current directory.
Read it and do the following:

STEP 1 - READ THE FILE
Use Python with openpyxl and pandas to read the file.
Extract all rows from all sheets using these exact column names:
- Step ID
- Test Scenario
- Action Performed
- Input/Configuration
- Expected Outcome
- Actual Outcome
- Result (Pass/Fail)
- Observations/Errors
- Follow Up Next Steps
- Screenshots

Handle these cases cleanly:
- Skip completely empty rows
- If Screenshots column has a value treat it as "Screenshot attached"
- If any cell is NaN or empty replace with "N/A"
- Count total tests, total passed, total failed

STEP 2 - GENERATE DOCUMENT 1: QA PROOF DOCUMENT
Create a formal Word document (.docx) saved as output/QA_Testing_Proof.docx

Structure it exactly like this:

Title: Quality Assurance Testing Report
Subtitle: Testing Proof of Execution
Date: [today's date]
Prepared by: QA Team
Status: [PASS if all passed, CONDITIONAL PASS if some failed, FAIL if majority failed]

Section 1 - Executive Sign-Off Summary
A table with:
| Total Test Cases | Total Passed | Total Failed | Pass Rate | Overall Status |

Section 2 - Test Coverage Overview
Short paragraph summarising what was tested, what systems or 
features were covered based on the test scenarios in the data.

Section 3 - Detailed Test Results
A full table with ALL rows from the Excel using these columns:
Step ID | Test Scenario | Result | Observations/Errors | Follow Up Next Steps

Section 4 - Failed Tests Detail
Only show failed tests here with full detail:
Step ID, Test Scenario, Action Performed, Input/Configuration,
Expected Outcome, Actual Outcome, Observations/Errors, Follow Up Next Steps

If no failures write: "All test cases passed successfully."

Section 5 - Risk Assessment
Based on the failed tests and observations, list potential risks.
If no failures write: "No risks identified. All scenarios passed."

Section 6 - Sign Off
A formal sign-off table with empty fields for:
QA Lead signature, Dev Lead signature, Date, Comments

STEP 3 - GENERATE DOCUMENT 2: MANAGEMENT SUMMARY
Create a Word document saved as output/Management_Summary.docx

Structure it exactly like this:

Title: Testing Summary Report
Subtitle: Management Overview
Date: [today's date]

Section 1 - At a Glance
3 sentence plain English summary of what was tested and the outcome.
No technical jargon.

Section 2 - Key Numbers
A clean simple table:
| What | Number |
| Total Scenarios Tested | X |
| Passed | X |
| Failed | X |
| Pass Rate | X% |
| Pending Follow Up | X |

Section 3 - What Was Tested
Bullet points in plain English describing the test scenarios.
No technical steps. Just what feature or flow was being verified.

Section 4 - Key Findings
- What worked well (based on passed tests)
- What needs attention (based on failed tests and observations)
- What needs follow up (based on Follow Up Next Steps column)

Section 5 - Go / No-Go Recommendation
A clear bold recommendation:
GO - if pass rate is above 90%
CONDITIONAL GO - if pass rate is 70-90% with explanation of conditions
NO GO - if pass rate is below 70% with explanation

Section 6 - Next Steps
Numbered list of actions needed before or after release
based strictly on the Follow Up Next Steps column data.

STEP 4 - FORMAT BOTH DOCUMENTS PROPERLY
For both Word documents:
- Use professional fonts: Title in 20pt bold, headers in 14pt bold, 
  body in 11pt
- Add a thin border to all tables
- Alternate row shading on tables (white and light grey)
- Make PASS cells green, FAIL cells red in the results table
- Add a page break between major sections
- Add page numbers in the footer
- Add the document title and date in the header

STEP 5 - CONFIRM OUTPUT
After saving both files print:
"✅ QA_Testing_Proof.docx saved to output/"
"✅ Management_Summary.docx saved to output/"
"📊 Summary: X tests | X passed | X failed | X% pass rate"
